From 4f32e543240d277b696f60a0fa0dee3a6e6f7bc0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ljhhkx529 <ljhzyx520@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 18:28:23 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 12/15] mesa: Turnip honours the kernel's command-stream
 capabilities

The GPU driver in the kernel publishes two capabilities the command stream has
to know about: whether the device runs a single render thread, and whether it
can wait for memory writes. Turnip ignored both, and on this board the command
processor rejected the submissions that assumed otherwise.

The patch makes the emitter honour the two bits:

- CP_WAIT_MEM_WRITES is substituted only for a zero payload and only when the
  kernel asks for it, with the legal zero-payload CP_NOP encoding (checked
  against the PM4 kit the kernel itself uses).
- The thread control is built in one place, and a device that runs one render
  thread gets the BR form with concurrent binning disabled. Every site that
  used to build the packet itself goes through that one place, and the BR
  patchpoint is left out on such a device.

The capabilities are read from the published device-info flags word, not from a
board name, so the same driver serves a runtime that needs neither.

scripts/check-turnip-fuxi-command-policy.py is what can be proved without a
GPU: the recorded patch set reconstructs the pinned Mesa source byte for byte,
the two hooks are gated on the capability accessors and on the packet kinds, the
payload rewrite touches only the thread selector and SYNC_THREADS and keeps
every other bit, and the substituted header is the legal CP_NOP encoding.
